  <p>Everyone has a map of consciousness. It is mostly unwritten, an intuitive guide to the states of mind we can inhabit. You know the feeling of a strong coffee, the pleasant disengagement of a glass of wine, the weird logic of a dream. We navigate these states without much thought. But can we make the map more precise? Can we create a cartography of the mind itself?</p>

  <h2 class="gradient-text">A First Map: The 2D Plane of Experience</h2>

  <p>Let's start with two fundamental axes of our daily lives. The first axis is our level of arousal, from being deeply asleep to fully awake. Let's call it the <strong>Awake/Asleep</strong> axis. The second is our level of focus, from being completely absorbed in a task to being mentally disengaged from the world. We can call this the <strong>Engaged/Disengaged</strong> axis.</p>
  
  <p>Combining these creates a simple 2D map with four quadrants, each containing familiar states:</p>
  
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Awake and Engaged:</strong> This is the quadrant of focus and activity. A morning coffee, the flow state of deep work, or an intense conversation live here.</li>
    <li><strong>Awake and Disengaged:</strong> Here we find states of relaxed distraction. Daydreaming, the gentle buzz from alcohol, or simply letting your mind wander. Dissociative states also fall in this category.</li>
    <li><strong>Asleep and Engaged:</strong> This is the world of dreams. Your body is asleep, but your mind is actively constructing narratives and vivid experiences.</li>
    <li><strong>Asleep and Disengaged:</strong> Deep, dreamless sleep. A state of pure rest where conscious experience seems to fade entirely. Anesthesia would be an extreme point on this part of the map.</li>
  </ul>

  <div id="plot-2d-container" class="cyber-card">
    <svg id="plot-2d" viewBox="0 0 400 300"></svg>
  </div>

  <h2 class="gradient-text">The Third Axis: From Order to Chaos</h2>

  <p>This 2D map is useful, but incomplete. It cannot distinguish between a productive flow state and a panic attack. Both are "Awake and Engaged", yet they feel fundamentally different. We are missing a dimension.</p>
  
  <p>Let's add a third axis: <strong>Order/Chaos</strong>. This is not about whether an experience is good or bad (a separate dimension of valence, or pleasure/pain). Instead, it measures the perceived structure of your conscious experience. Is your thinking linear, logical, and stable? Or is it fragmented, recursive, and fluid? High order corresponds to states of deep meditation (Jhana) or intense focus. High chaos is the domain of psychosis, sensory overload, and, as we will see, psychedelics.</p>

  <h2 class="gradient-text">Mapping the Uncharted Realms</h2>

  <p>With three axes, our map can now place a much wider range of states. A state is no longer a point in a plane, but a location in a 3D space: (Engagement, Arousal, Order).</p>
  
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Meditation:</strong> High arousal, high engagement, high order.</li>
    <li><strong>Cannabis:</strong> High arousal, mild disengagement, moderate chaos.</li>
    <li><strong>MDMA:</strong> High arousal, very high engagement, high order (often with a strong pro-social, warm character).</li>
    <li><strong>Ketamine:</strong> Low arousal, high disengagement, high chaos (a dissociative, dream-like state).</li>
    <li><strong>LSD / Psilocybin:</strong> High arousal, high engagement, and extremely high chaos.</li>
  </ul>
  
  <p>Psychedelics like LSD are interesting because they push the mind into a region of this state-space that is not normally accessible. They create a state that is simultaneously highly awake and engaged, but also profoundly chaotic.</p>

  <div id="plot-3d-container" class="cyber-card">
    <div id="plot-3d"></div>
  </div>
  
  <h2 class="gradient-text">The Phenomenology of an Acid Trip</h2>

  <p>What does it feel like to be in this corner of the map? My own experiences, viewed through this framework, reveal a few key phenomenological markers.</p>

  <p><strong>Time Distortion:</strong> The "Engaged" axis turns on itself. You become engaged with the feeling of engagement. Thoughts become recursive loops. A second can feel like an eternity because it is packed with so much self-referential metadata.</p>
  
  <p><strong>Ego Dissolution:</strong> The sense of a stable, located self dissolves. Your coordinates on the map become uncertain. The "I" that is having the experience feels less like a point and more like a diffuse cloud, or sometimes, disappears entirely.</p>
  
  <p><strong>Geometric Visuals:</strong> The "Order/Chaos" axis begins to oscillate. The structure of your sensory input fluctuates rapidly, creating the perception of intricate, geometric patterns overlaid on reality. The world breathes.</p>
  
  <p><strong>Synesthesia:</strong> The axes start to bleed into one another. Sounds have colors, colors have textures, emotions have shapes. The clean separation between sensory modalities breaks down.</p>

  <p>The arc of the experience, from the come-up to the peak and back down, can be visualized as a trajectory through this 3D space. It starts at baseline, climbs towards maximum chaos, and then slowly returns to a more ordered state.</p>

  <div id="lsd-trajectory-container" class="cyber-card">
    <div id="lsd-trajectory"></div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="gradient-text">Why Build a Map?</h2>

  <p>This is just one model, a way to structure thinking about these states. It is not the territory itself. But having a map, even a provisional one, is useful. It allows us to compare experiences, to ask better questions, and to see relationships we might have missed. For psychedelic therapy, for understanding the varieties of suffering, and even for thinking about the potential for consciousness in AI, building a cartography of the mind is a necessary step.</p>
  
  <style>
    .cyber-card {
      background: rgba(16, 16, 24, 0.6);
      border: 1px solid #333;
      border-radius: 8px;
      padding: 1rem;
      margin: 2rem 0;
      backdrop-filter: blur(10px);
    }
    #plot-2d-container, #plot-3d-container, #lsd-trajectory-container {
      width: 100%;
      height: 350px;
      position: relative;
    }
    #plot-3d, #lsd-trajectory {
      width: 100%;
      height: 100%;
    }
    svg text {
      font-family: 'monospace';
      font-size: 10px;
      fill: #8888aa;
    }
    svg .axis-label {
      font-size: 12px;
      fill: #b44aff;
    }
    svg .point-label {
      fill: #00f0ff;
    }
  </style>
  
  <script is:inline>
    // 2D Plot
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
      const svg = document.getElementById('plot-2d');
      const width = 400;
      const height = 300;
      const margin = { top: 20, right: 20, bottom: 40, left: 40 };
      const x = (val) => margin.left + (val + 1) / 2 * (width - margin.left - margin.right);
      const y = (val) => height - margin.bottom - (val + 1) / 2 * (height - margin.top - margin.bottom);

      const points = [
        { label: "Coffee", x: 0.7, y: 0.8 },
        { label: "Flow State", x: 0.9, y: 0.7 },
        { label: "Alcohol", x: -0.5, y: 0.6 },
        { label: "Daydreaming", x: -0.3, y: 0.5 },
        { label: "Vivid Dream", x: 0.5, y: -0.7 },
        { label: "Deep Sleep", x: -0.8, y: -0.8 },
        { label: "Meditation", x: 0.6, y: 0.6 },
      ];

      // Axes
      svg.innerHTML = `
        <line x1="${margin.left}" y1="${height - margin.bottom}" x2="${width - margin.right}" y2="${height - margin.bottom}" stroke="#333" />
        <line x1="${margin.left + (width - margin.left - margin.right)/2}" y1="${margin.top}" x2="${margin.left + (width - margin.left - margin.right)/2}" y2="${height - margin.bottom}" stroke="#333" />
        <text x="${width - margin.right}" y="${height - margin.bottom + 15}" text-anchor="end" class="axis-label">Engaged →</text>
        <text x="${margin.left}" y="${height - margin.bottom + 15}" text-anchor="start" class="axis-label">← Disengaged</text>
        <text x="${margin.left + (width - margin.left - margin.right)/2}" y="${margin.top - 5}" text-anchor="middle" class="axis-label">↑ Awake</text>
        <text x="${margin.left + (width - margin.left - margin.right)/2}" y="${height - margin.bottom + 25}" text-anchor="middle" class="axis-label">↓ Asleep</text>
      `;

      // Points
      points.forEach(p => {
        const circle = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "circle");
        circle.setAttribute("cx", x(p.x));
        circle.setAttribute("cy", y(p.y));
        circle.setAttribute("r", 4);
        circle.setAttribute("fill", "#b44aff");
        svg.appendChild(circle);

        const text = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "text");
        text.setAttribute("x", x(p.x) + 5);
        text.setAttribute("y", y(p.y) + 4);
        text.textContent = p.label;
        text.classList.add("point-label");
        svg.appendChild(text);
      });
    });
  </script>
  
  <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/p5.js/1.9.0/p5.min.js"></script>
  <script is:inline>
      // 3D Plot
      const sketch3d = (p) => {
        const points = [
            { label: "LSD", pos: p.createVector(0.8, 0.9, 0.9), color: '#ff4b4b' },
            { label: "MDMA", pos: p.createVector(0.9, 0.8, -0.7), color: '#4bfff3' },
            { label: "Cannabis", pos: p.createVector(-0.4, 0.7, 0.5), color: '#4bff63' },
            { label: "Ketamine", pos: p.createVector(-0.8, -0.5, 0.7), color: '#f34bff' },
            { label: "Meditation", pos: p.createVector(0.6, 0.6, -0.8), color: '#ffffff' },
            { label: "Baseline", pos: p.createVector(0, 0, 0), color: '#8888aa' },
        ];
        let angle = 0;

        p.setup = () => {
            const container = document.getElementById('plot-3d-container');
            p.createCanvas(container.offsetWidth, container.offsetHeight, p.WEBGL).parent('plot-3d');
            p.textFont('monospace');
        };

        p.draw = () => {
            p.background(10, 10, 15);
            p.rotateY(angle);
            p.rotateX(0.5);

            // Axes
            p.stroke(51, 51, 51);
            p.strokeWeight(1);
            p.line(-150, 0, 0, 150, 0, 0); // X: Engagement
            p.line(0, -150, 0, 0, 150, 0); // Y: Arousal
            p.line(0, 0, -150, 0, 0, 150); // Z: Chaos

            // Points
            points.forEach(pt => {
                p.push();
                const x = p.map(pt.pos.x, -1, 1, -120, 120);
                const y = p.map(pt.pos.y, -1, 1, 120, -120); // Invert Y for screen
                const z = p.map(pt.pos.z, -1, 1, -120, 120);
                p.translate(x, y, z);
                p.fill(pt.color);
                p.noStroke();
                p.sphere(4);
                p.pop();
            });

            angle += 0.005;
        };

         p.windowResized = () => {
            const container = document.getElementById('plot-3d-container');
            p.resizeCanvas(container.offsetWidth, container.offsetHeight);
        };
      };
      new p5(sketch3d);

      // Trajectory Plot
      const sketchTrajectory = (p) => {
        const trajectoryPoints = [
            p.createVector(0, 0, 0),       // T+0
            p.createVector(0.4, 0.5, 0.3), // T+1
            p.createVector(0.8, 0.9, 0.9), // T+3 (Peak)
            p.createVector(0.6, 0.7, 0.5), // T+6
            p.createVector(0.2, 0.4, 0.2), // T+8
            p.createVector(0, 0.1, 0.1)      // T+12
        ];
        let angle = 0;

        p.setup = () => {
            const container = document.getElementById('lsd-trajectory-container');
            p.createCanvas(container.offsetWidth, container.offsetHeight, p.WEBGL).parent('lsd-trajectory');
        };

        p.draw = () => {
            p.background(10, 10, 15);
            p.rotateY(angle);
            p.rotateX(0.5);

             // Axes
            p.stroke(51, 51, 51);
            p.strokeWeight(1);
            p.line(-150, 0, 0, 150, 0, 0); 
            p.line(0, -150, 0, 0, 150, 0);
            p.line(0, 0, -150, 0, 0, 150);

            // Trajectory
            p.noFill();
            p.stroke('#00f0ff');
            p.strokeWeight(2);
            p.beginShape();
            trajectoryPoints.forEach(pt => {
                const x = p.map(pt.x, -1, 1, -120, 120);
                const y = p.map(pt.y, -1, 1, 120, -120);
                const z = p.map(pt.z, -1, 1, -120, 120);
                p.vertex(x, y, z);
            });
            p.endShape();

            // Draw points on trajectory
            trajectoryPoints.forEach((pt, i) => {
                 p.push();
                 const x = p.map(pt.x, -1, 1, -120, 120);
                 const y = p.map(pt.y, -1, 1, 120, -120);
                 const z = p.map(pt.z, -1, 1, -120, 120);
                 p.translate(x, y, z);
                 p.fill('#b44aff');
                 p.noStroke();
                 p.sphere(i === 2 ? 5 : 3); // Emphasize peak
                 p.pop();
            });

            angle += 0.005;
        };

         p.windowResized = () => {
            const container = document.getElementById('lsd-trajectory-container');
            p.resizeCanvas(container.offsetWidth, container.offsetHeight);
        };
      };
      new p5(sketchTrajectory);
  </script>
